id: records/2026-09-04-yawnbot-agentic-concept-embodiment-clarification
title: "Yawnbots as consistent agentic embodiments of concepts and other Yawn holons"
kind: clarification-record
status: proposed-canonical-backfill
recorded: 2026-09-04
supersedes_history: false
mutationAuthorized: false
externalEffectsAuthorized: false

source_observation: >
  Dave clarified that the game should let people experience Yawnbots as Agents
  and, in an important human-facing sense, as the concepts themselves: meaningful
  distinctions should be able to receive recognizable robot bodies, voices,
  state, relationships, and behavior so the user can talk with them, navigate
  them, and program them consistently. This is intended as a gamified AI-native
  psychotechnology rather than a collection of unrelated assistant widgets.

pressure_test_result:
  preserve: >
    The intuition is product-defining: a concept or other meaningful holon can
    become interactable by receiving a Yawnbot embodiment, and every Yawnbot
    should teach the same core behavioral grammar.
  correct: >
    Do not literally collapse concept, Yawn, visible bot body, operational Agent,
    intelligence provider, capability, or authority. The product may say "give
    the concept a bot body" while the protocol preserves the distinctions needed
    for safety, portability, replay, and truthful attribution.

clarified_layers:
  concept_or_semantic_center: >
    What the bot is about and the stable symbolic identity it helps make
    navigable. A semantic center may also be a relationship, objective, question,
    role, Arena, or composite holon.
  yawn: >
    The situated contract and durable state around that semantic center.
  yawnbot_embodiment: >
    The persistent game-facing character: icon/body, name, address, state, voice,
    controls, time, provisioning, and aperture.
  steward_agent: >
    The attributed human or software Agent currently operating through the
    embodiment. A sleeping Yawnbot may have no active steward.
  intelligence: >
    The replaceable reasoning capability connected to the runtime.
  capabilities_and_authority: >
    What can technically be done and what has actually been authorized remain
    separate.

product_language:
  primary: "A Yawnbot is a Yawn given an interactable body and bounded agency."
  concept_case: "Give a concept a bot body."
  deeper: >
    The visible Yawnbot is the place where the person experiences a semantic
    holon as a character-like Agent; the protocol beneath it prevents character
    identity from being mistaken for consciousness, truth, consent, or authority.

programming_thesis:
  statement: >
    YAWN.bot is a game-like programming environment. Natural conversation and
    direct manipulation should compile into inspectable proposals affecting a
    bot's semantic center, purpose, relationships, instructions, skills,
    boundaries, attention, provisioning, authority, and proof conditions.
  user_experience:
    - "Talk to the bot."
    - "See what it thinks it heard."
    - "Correct or connect the concepts and relationships."
    - "Equip capabilities."
    - "Grant only the authority intended."
    - "Set what should wake it and what would count as proof."
    - "Run, pause, inspect, and replay it."
  boundary: >
    Programming by dialogue remains a proposed change until the relevant
    semantic, structural, privacy, or authority gate is satisfied.

consistent_behavior:
  requirement: >
    Every Yawnbot must feel like the same kind of game object. Domain knowledge,
    body, personality, model provider, and tools may vary, but the meaning of
    state, controls, attribution, authority, consequence, proof, and replay may
    not drift from bot to bot.
  kernel:
    - locate
    - listen
    - symbolize
    - orient
    - hold
    - deliberate
    - request_authority
    - move_or_wait
    - observe_consequence
    - evaluate_proof
    - update
    - sleep_or_wake
  desired_transfer: >
    After learning one Yawnbot, the user should know how to talk with, inspect,
    correct, hold, authorize, pause, and replay any other Yawnbot.

relation_to_attention_frontier:
  local_agent_role: >
    A Yawnbot can advocate for the relevance of the concept, relationship, or
    objective it stewards by submitting an attributed local attention claim.
  root_boundary: >
    Local advocacy does not make the bot the global decision-maker. The root
    Yawn compares claims in relationship to the principal's accepted values,
    constraints, time, evidence, safety, and authority.
  challenger_boundary: >
    Advocate and challenger are normally temporary deliberation roles, not fixed
    opposing personalities or automatically persistent Yawnbots.

important_codebase_drift_to_detect:
  observation: >
    Any implementation that labels orientation dimensions or UI modules as
    independent Agents merely because they have separate panels risks confusing
    a lens with an Agent. Outcome, tension, model, experiment, and coherence can
    be capabilities or apertures of one Yawnbot unless each has its own persistent
    identity, memory, responsibility, capability boundary, and authority.
  required_check: >
    Search UI, schemas, prompts, and agent registries for hard-coded special
    agent types, incompatible bot controls, duplicate semantic identities,
    personality-as-identity, provider-coupled state, or visual states that imply
    authority not present in the underlying record.

psychotechnology_hypothesis:
  status: unproven_research_and_product_hypothesis
  claim: >
    Stable agentic embodiment may make externalized cognition easier to
    recognize, remember, navigate, program, compare, and act through than plain
    chat, static notes, or an unembodied graph.
  test: >
    Compare embodied and unembodied projections while holding model capability
    and underlying semantic state constant. Measure navigation transfer,
    correction cost, recall, context restoration, next-move clarity, authority
    comprehension, cognitive carrying reduction, and unwanted anthropomorphic or
    compulsive effects.

anti_collapse_rules:
  - "concept != Agent"
  - "concept != Yawn"
  - "Yawn != visible bot body"
  - "visible bot body != active steward Agent"
  - "Yawnbot character != intelligence provider"
  - "animation != agency"
  - "personality != identity"
  - "local advocacy != principal authority"
  - "gamification != engagement maximization"
  - "a new concept does not automatically create a Yawnbot"

implementation_direction:
  - >
    Define one reusable Yawnbot shell/body bound to canonical Yawn, Agent,
    provider, capability, authority, time, provisioning, and replay references.
  - >
    Replace or reinterpret hard-coded pseudo-agent panels as skills, lenses, or
    bounded workers unless they satisfy the Agent persistence threshold.
  - >
    Give every bot the same core state machine and interaction operators while
    allowing accepted skins, voices, skills, and domain-specific Views.
  - >
    Show whether the bot is sleeping, stewarded, deliberating, awaiting
    authority, acting, waiting on reality, evaluating proof, or blocked.
  - >
    Let concepts and relationships receive agentic embodiment only when the
    persistence and agency value exceeds orchestration and cognitive cost.

proof_questions:
  - "Does embodiment make the user's symbolic model more navigable than the same structure rendered as plain cards?"
  - "Can the user predict a new bot's controls after learning one bot?"
  - "Can a provider be replaced without changing bot identity, state, permissions, or behavior-kernel semantics?"
  - "Can a concept participate in several Yawns without duplicate concept or bot identities?"
  - "Do users understand that a character-like bot may be sleeping, uncertain, unauthorized, or wrong?"
  - "Does the game improve reality-facing agency rather than merely increasing interaction time?"

